WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.660
A service is logic, policies

2
00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.000
— and configuration.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.640
Most teams treat that third one as an afterthought.

4
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.930
Chenile doesn't.

5
00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:13.140
Meet cconfig.

6
00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:17.060
Every module owns a JSON file, named after it.

7
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.440
A value can be a simple string, or a deeply nested object.

8
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.350
Configuration lives right next to the module that owns it.

9
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.290
The headline feature:

10
00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:28.390
override any value

11
00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:29.490
— down to the path

12
00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.540
— at runtime.

13
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.130
Override key1.

14
00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:33.890
Override just key2.abc

15
00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.260
— and leave the rest intact.

16
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.880
Change the range inside field1, from 100 to 101.

17
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.790
Or add a whole new field2.

18
00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.530
No editing the base file.

19
00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:45.620
No redeploy.

20
00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.760
Overrides can apply to every request

21
00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.670
— or only when a custom attribute matches.

22
00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:53.990
In SaaS, that's the tenant ID.

23
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.190
Same request, two tenants, two effective values.

24
00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.950
The base config stays shared and clean.

25
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.640
Your code reads config through one client:

26
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:04.650
CconfigClient.

27
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.370
Behind it:

28
00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.560
properties, JSON, environment, or a database

29
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.050
— with a memory cache in front.

30
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.670
Start with JSON files.

31
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:16.600
Later, move overrides into a database for a live admin UI

32
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.020
— without touching the code that reads config.

33
00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:21.900
Same move as always:

34
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.820
depend on the client interface,

35
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.470
and choose the source by configuration.

36
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:27.990
Next episode:

37
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.120
the service registry and proxies

38
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.170
— client-side interception.
